REGIONAL REGULATION OF SUKOHARJO REGENCY

NUMBER 6 OF 2011

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR VICTIMS OF GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF SUKOHARJO,

Considering:

a. that all forms of violence, especially gender-based violence and violence against children are violations of human rights and crimes against human dignity as well as forms of discrimination;
b. that victims of gender-based violence and children must receive protection, both from the regional government and/or the community in order to be protected and free from violence and/or threats of violence within the scope of the household and community;
c. that based on Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection and Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence, the Regional Government together with the community is obliged to make efforts to prevent, protect, and restore victims of gender-based violence and children;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children;

In view of:

1. Law Number 13 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions within the Province of Central Java;
2. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention on The Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 3 of 1997 concerning Juvenile Court (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1997 Number 3, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3668);
5. Law Number 20 of 1999 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention Number 138 concerning Minimum Age for Admission to Employment (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835);
6. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
7. Law Number 1 of 2000 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention Number 182 concerning the Prohibition and Immediate Action for the Elimination of the Worst Forms of Child Labour (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2000 Number 30, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3941);
8. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
9. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4281);
10. Law Number 10 of 2004 concerning the Establishment of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 53, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4389);
11. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
12. Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 125, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437 as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 12 of 2008 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 59, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
13. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
14. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
15. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4967);
16. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 144, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);
17. Government Regulation Number 2 of 1988 concerning Child Welfare Efforts for Children with Problems (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1988 Number 2, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 3367);
18. Government Regulation Number 6 of 1988 concerning Coordination of Vertical Agency Activities in the Regions (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1988 Number 10, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 3373);
19. Government Regulation Number 79 of 2005 concerning Guidelines for the Development and Supervision of Regional Government Administration (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2005 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4593);
20. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
21. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning the Division of Government Affairs Between the Government, Provincial Regional Government, and Regency/City Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
22. Government Regulation Number 50 of 2007 concerning Procedures for the Implementation of Regional Cooperation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 112, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4761);
23. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
24. Presidential Regulation Number 1 of 2007 concerning Ratification, Promulgation and Dissemination of Legislation;
25. Regional Regulation of Central Java Province Number 3 of 2009 Concerning the Implementation of Protection for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children (Regional Gazette of the Province of 2009 Number 3, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Central Java Province Number 20);
26. Regional Regulation of Sukoharjo Regency Number 1 of 2008 concerning Government Affairs that are the Authority of the Regional Government of Sukoharjo Regency (Regional Gazette of Sukoharjo Regency of 2008 Number 1, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Sukoharjo Regency Number 155);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SUKOHARJO REGENCY

and

REGENT OF SUKOHARJO

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR VICTIMS OF GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE AND CHILDREN.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of victims of gender-based violence and children is based on Pancasila and grounded in the 1945 Constitution, as well as basic principles that include:
a. non-discrimination;
b. best interests of the victim;
c. gender justice and equality;
d. victim protection;
e. maternal survival;
f. child survival and development;
g. respect for the child's opinion;
h. transparency;
i. integration;
j. not blaming the victim;
k. victim confidentiality; and
l. decision-making in the hands of the victim.

Article 3

The objectives of protection of victims of gender-based violence and children are:
a. to prevent all forms of gender-based violence and violence against children, occurring within the scope of the household and/or community;
b. to provide protection;
c. to provide legal assistance;
d. to pursue recovery and social reintegration; and
e. to provide a sense of security to victims.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 4

1. Every victim of gender-based violence and child victim of violence has the right to:
a. obtain protection for the security of themselves, their family, and their property, and be free from threats related to information that will be, is being, or has been given;
b. participate in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;
c. obtain information regarding case developments and court decisions;
d. obtain integrated services that are fast, appropriate, comfortable, and in accordance with needs;
e. recovery and social reintegration;
f. obtain legal, psychological, spiritual, economic, social assistance and interpreters;
g. special handling related to victim confidentiality;
h. obtain guarantees of their rights related to their status as wife/mother, husband/father, child and household member as well as community member; and
i. obtain information about laws and regulations that protect victims.
2. The rights of victims as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND AUTHORITIES OF REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

Article 5

1. In providing protection to victims of gender-based violence and children, the Regional Government has the obligation to:
a. implement all efforts to prevent the occurrence of violence;
b. provide protection for victims of violence;
c. provide recovery and social reintegration services for victims;
d. encourage and increase community participation;
e. cooperate with service providers in efforts to prevent, protect and recover victims of violence; and
f. supervise the implementation of services for victims involving the community.
2. To carry out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government has the authority to:
a. formulate policies and programs on the elimination of gender-based violence and violence against children;
b. facilitate the implementation of integrated services;
c. provide facilities and infrastructure;
d. increase the capacity of service provider institutions;
e. coordinate and cooperate in the implementation of protection for victims of gender-based violence and violence against children; and
f. conduct monitoring and evaluation.
3. The regional government in carrying out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) must pay attention to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands, wives or other persons who are legally responsible for the victim.

Article 8

The duties of integrated services as referred to in Article 6 and Article 7 are:
a. to seek prevention;
b. recovery and social reintegration;
c. to provide legal protection;
d. to coordinate and cooperate;
e. to seek increased community participation; and
f. monitoring and reporting.

Article 9

In carrying out the duties as referred to in Article 8, integrated services apply the following principles:
a. no fees charged for underprivileged families;
b. fast;
c. safe;
d. empathy;
e. non-discrimination;
f. easily accessible;
g. confidential; and
h. integrated

Article 11

Recovery and Social Reintegration Efforts as referred to in Article 8 letter b, include:
a. providing physical recovery at health service institutions;
b. providing medicolegal services;
c. assisting the repatriation of victims;
d. providing temporary protection in safe houses (shelters);
e. providing psychosocial recovery and assistance;
f. providing religious guidance services; and
g. preparing families and communities, economic empowerment, and return to school and/or other educational institutions.

Article 13

Coordination and cooperation as referred to in Article 8 letter d include:
a. coordinating and cooperating in handling violence cases with district/city integrated services;
b. coordinating and cooperating with integrated services between districts;
c. coordinating and cooperating with service provider institutions for victims of gender-based violence and violence against children; and
d. coordinating and consulting with the Regional Government.

Article 14

Community participation as referred to in Article 8 letter e is carried out through:
a. providing protection for victims;
b. providing emergency assistance;
c. providing advocacy to victims and or the community regarding the handling of gender-based violence and violence against children cases;
d. assisting the process of submitting applications for protection orders;
e. conveying information to authorized officials related to cases of gender-based violence and violence against children;
f. fostering local wisdom in handling gender-based violence and violence against children;
g. organizing the strengthening of community groups in handling gender-based violence and violence against children;
h. disseminating information about laws and regulations related to gender-based violence and violence against children;
i. providing protection for victims;
j. providing emergency assistance; and
k. assisting the process of submitting applications for protection orders.
